    summary: `Here's something worth knowing before you get further in: this isn't one battle, it's three, and the text is literally organized that way. Old commentators even gave the three sections a shared name for what each one is supposedly untying: a "knot" (granthi) — as if something in a person gets tied up tight in three different places, and each section of the story loosens one of them.

The first, shortest section deals with two demons, Madhu and Kaiṭabha — the pull to do nothing, and the voice that argues change won't work anyway. Call this the "dullness" knot: the most basic, most universal form of getting stuck.

The second section deals with one demon, Mahiṣāsura, the shapeshifting ego — beat it in one form and it just returns wearing another. Call this the "ego" knot: harder, because it doesn't just resist you, it disguises itself as you.

The third section is by far the longest — nearly two-thirds of the whole text — and deals with Śumbha and Niśumbha, the quiet sense of "I'm in charge" and "this is mine." Call this the "pride" knot: hardest of all, precisely because at this point it doesn't look like an obstacle anymore. It looks like your ordinary, ambient sense of being a person with a life.

The practical point in laying it out this way: real change isn't a single dramatic victory after which you're simply "done." It's rounds, and each round gets subtler and harder to even recognize as a round, even as the demons on the page get scarier-looking. The last fight is the hardest exactly because it's the least obvious — which is why the text spends more time on it than on the first two combined.`,
