    title: 'A Happy Ending for Both of Them (Nārāyaṇī Stuti)',
    category: 'How the story ends',
    summary: `With the last demons defeated, the gods sing one final hymn of thanks, and the story returns to where it started: the forest, with King Suratha and the merchant Samādhi, who have spent three years there practicing patiently.

The Goddess appears to both of them and asks what they want. This ending is worth knowing because of what happens next — she doesn't tell them the "spiritual" answer is the only correct one.

Suratha, still representing practical, worldly engagement, asks for his kingdom back, better and more secure than before. Samādhi, representing the seeker who wants to be free of craving altogether, asks only for wisdom — the kind that ends the cycle of attachment and suffering for good.

She grants both wishes, fully, without ranking one above the other. That's the whole text's closing statement, and it's a more interesting one than "renounce the world and everything will be fine": a genuinely good life has room for both real engagement with ordinary goals — a career, a family, security — and a real inner life that isn't consumed by anxious attachment to any of it. The tradition actually names four legitimate aims of a human life — duty, success, enjoyment, and freedom — and this hymn praises the Goddess as the source of all four, not just the last one.

Put simply: this story doesn't end by telling you to leave your life behind and go meditate in a cave. It ends by saying you can want your kingdom back and still be free — those two things were never actually in competition.

Satyadev, in Sadhan-Samar, reads the two boons as the anatomy of every prayer. Beneath Suratha's kingdom and Samadhi's release lie the two hidden asks every living being carries to the Mother whether knowingly or not: lordship (aishvarya - all-powerfulness) and pure knowing (vishuddha-bodha). She answers across lifetimes, purifying birth after birth, fitting each soul for the fitness it lacks. The king's "own kingdom" (svarajya) is, inwardly, mastery over mind, intellect and senses - where saying "I" once meant slavery to them, now "I" recalls Her and they go dim: that is Her first gift. Her further gift, Manu-hood, is establishment in collective human consciousness itself - becoming the Sun's son means merging into the vast life-power, seated in the lap of Savarna-shakti who makes, keeps and dissolves; and the blessings such Manus rain down are what turn mankind, day by day, toward the thirst for wisdom and glory.

Hence the rule the book draws in a quoted verse: if you must worship, worship the Great - for the Great gives beyond the asking. Suratha came for a kingdom and was handed, with it, Manu-hood, the supremely blissful station. So it is in this world too: a man first takes refuge over some petty worldly lack, and that lack duly vanishes - but by grace he also gains fitness for incomparable things, wisdom and dispassion. It was exactly so with the boy Dhruva. To the merchant, meanwhile, She grants knowing itself, and through knowing, perfection-release (samsiddhi, liberation) - for She is the wish-tree, giving unhesitatingly whatever is asked of Her in true knowledge. And both seekers, the book notes, mirror an older pair: one sat at Krishna's head and carried off His glory-army of enjoyment, the other sat at His feet and won the Lord Himself as his charioteer - one entranced by divine majesty, the other in love with divine sweetness. Both moods are all there is to soul-hood, and She, smiling, granting wishes and fears' removal, waits with eager eyes for the child to simply stand before Her and ask as a child asks its mother.

The hymn that earns this ending works the same way in miniature. Satyadev, in Sadhan-Samar, reads the Narayani Stuti (chapter 11) as a three-rung ladder built from a single refrain-word: sharanagata-dina-arta - the surrendered, the lowly, the anguished. Each rung ripens into one establishing (pratishtha), and each maps to one strand of nature. Surrender (sharanagata) - laying down every other duty and taking Her alone as refuge - establishes the seeker in Being itself (sat-pratishtha): this is Her sattvic face. Lowliness (dina) - the smallness one feels beholding Her universe-filling glory, "how poor am I" - establishes him in Life everywhere (prana-pratishtha), seeing Her as consciousness in all breath: this is Her rajasic face, and paradoxically it is the very sense of Her grandeur that teaches one's own poverty. Anguish (arta) - the pining ache of Her absence, since nothing in this world is bliss and She alone is its store - establishes him in Bliss solid (ananda-pratishtha): this is Her tamasic face transfigured, restlessness ripened past itself into stillness.

So the prayer at the close - "first make us surrendered, lowly, anguished; then establish us in Being, in Life, in Bliss" - is not flattery but method. Until those three marks shine in the seeker, he cannot even hope to behold Her rescuer-form; the hymn therefore begs Her to make Her own unfit child fit for Her self-revelation. And the verse before it (11.10) states the metaphysics the ladder climbs: She leans on the three strands yet is made of them, one consciousness throbbing threefold as making, keeping and unmaking - and the prayer is to be led out of that threefold turning into Her ageless formless form, where no wheel turns at all.`,
