    title: 'She Promises to Come Back as Whatever the Moment Needs',
    category: 'How the story ends',
    summary: `Right after Śumbha and Niśumbha are defeated, the Goddess makes an unusual promise about the future: demons like them, she says, will rise again someday — and when they do, she'll come back to deal with it. But here's the part worth noticing: she doesn't promise to always show up the same way.

Sometimes it's ferocity again. But sometimes, strikingly, it's something else entirely: during a hundred-year famine, she promises to end it by literally growing vegetables and grain out of her own body until the rains finally return, earning the name Śākambharī, roughly "she who nourishes with plants." Other times she describes coming with a hundred watchful eyes (Śatākṣī), or as a literal swarm of countless bees bringing down a threat too large for any single fighter to handle alone (Bhrāmarī).

Read symbolically, this is one of the quietly radical ideas in the whole text: it flatly rejects the notion that help has to look one particular, heroic way. Sometimes what a crisis actually needs is ferocity. Sometimes it needs patient, exhausting nourishment — showing up day after day to literally feed people, with nothing dramatic about it at all. Sometimes it needs many small, coordinated efforts rather than one dramatic rescue. The promise isn't "I'll look a certain way when I return" — it's "whatever this specific situation actually calls for, that's the form I'll take."

That's a genuinely useful thing to remember about your own capacity to handle hard situations, too: the right response to one problem in your life might look nothing like the right response to another — decisive confrontation here, patient endurance there, quiet collective effort somewhere else — and that's not inconsistency. That's just fitting the response to the actual need in front of you, instead of insisting on one signature move for every occasion.`,
