    title: 'Why the Text Tells You to Read It Again (The Phalaśruti)',
    category: 'How the story ends',
    summary: `The second-to-last chapter does something a little unusual for a story: it stops the narrative entirely and directly tells the reader what reciting or hearing it is actually supposed to be good for, and when to turn to it. It lists an almost exhaustive catalogue of the disasters people genuinely fear — fire, robbers, imprisonment, being lost at sea, war, wracking illness, an enraged authority figure who wants you dead — and says that whoever remembers this story in the middle of a crisis like that will find a way through it. It goes on to recommend specific recurring days for regular recitation, rather than treating it as a one-time read.

Read symbolically, this is a genuinely practical point disguised as ritual instruction rather than tradition purely for tradition's sake. The value of a story like this was never supposed to be that you absorb its lesson once and you're done with it. Real crises almost never happen when you're calm enough to reason your way through a book's worth of wisdom from scratch. So the actual argument being made is this: if a story's core lesson — don't fight the confusing feeling alone, ask for help, remember what you already know — is going to be available to you in a genuine panic, at 3am, with your heart racing, it has to already be familiar. Rehearsed ahead of time, not looked up in the moment.

That's the real case for repetition, and it isn't superstitious: it's the same reason people run fire drills before there's ever an actual fire, and the same reason athletes and soldiers over-practice a response until it becomes reflex. You're not memorizing a story for its own sake — you're pre-loading a way of responding to panic, so it's already there, waiting, the one time you'll actually need it.`,
